egismoc: retry, don't fail, on an SDCP identify response that does not verify

On the Galaxy Book4 Pro 360 (Egis 1c7a:05a1) the first verify after fprintd
starts often fails with

    SDCP AuthorizedIdentity verification failed.
    Device reported an error during verify: Device SDCP Identify response
    was not valid

-- always within a second or two of the daemon starting, after an earlier
daemon was stopped mid-verify. The MAC is keyed by the session's
application secret, so the likeliest reading is a reply to the previous
session's request. FP_DEVICE_ERROR_DATA_INVALID ends the operation and
pam_fprintd falls back to the password although the finger matched.

Report FP_DEVICE_RETRY_GENERAL instead: fprintd asks for another scan and
keeps listening. A retry authorizes nothing; the next scan is verified
exactly as before.

--- a/libfprint/drivers/egismoc/egismoc.c	2026-10-07 18:18:22.762539016 +0900
+++ b/libfprint/drivers/egismoc/egismoc.c	2026-10-07 18:18:22.809538804 +0900
@@ -1518,14 +1518,27 @@
           return;
         }
 
-      /* Ensure the returned identity is valid per SDCP. */
+      /* Ensure the returned identity is valid per SDCP. A response that fails
+         the check proves nothing either way -- in practice it is the device
+         answering a request from an earlier session (the previous daemon was
+         stopped mid-verify), MACed with that session's secret -- so ask for
+         another scan instead of failing the whole operation: a hard error
+         here made pam_fprintd give up and fall back to the password at once.
+         A retry grants nothing; the next scan must verify like any other. */
       if (!fpi_sdcp_verify_authorized_identity (sdcp_dev, host_nonce,
                                                 enrollment_id, device_mac))
         {
-          fpi_ssm_mark_failed (self->task_ssm,
-                               fpi_device_error_new_msg (FP_DEVICE_ERROR_DATA_INVALID,
-                                                         "Device SDCP Identify "
-                                                         "response was not valid"));
+          fp_warn ("Device SDCP Identify response was not valid "
+                   "(stale session?), asking for another scan");
+
+          if (fpi_device_get_current_action (device) == FPI_DEVICE_ACTION_VERIFY)
+            fpi_device_verify_report (device, FPI_MATCH_ERROR, NULL,
+                                      fpi_device_retry_new (FP_DEVICE_RETRY_GENERAL));
+          else
+            fpi_device_identify_report (device, NULL, NULL,
+                                        fpi_device_retry_new (FP_DEVICE_RETRY_GENERAL));
+
+          fpi_ssm_next_state (self->task_ssm);
           return;
         }
 
